      "fact_text": {
        "text": "In an integer array, elements that are not explicitly initialized are set to 0 by default.",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "In a boolean array, elements default to false.",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "In a String array, elements default to an empty string.",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "The newer syntax for creating and initializing an array uses curly braces with the items listed inside, eliminating the need for the new operator.",
        "refs": ["Q0001"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "An array’s length can be read using the .length field.",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Java arrays have a fixed size; once created, items cannot be added or removed.",
        "refs": ["Q0002", "Q0003"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "To obtain a dynamically sized collection, one should use one of the collection classes instead of an array.",
        "refs": ["Q0003"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Arrays can be sorted using the Arrays.sort method, which is overloaded for different parameter types.",
        "refs": ["Q0003", "Q0004"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Multi‑dimensional arrays (e.g., two‑dimensional or three‑dimensional) can be created to store matrices or cube data, useful in scientific computations.",
        "refs": ["Q0004"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    }
  ],
